  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe Long Term portion of the forecast continues to look active for \nwest Texas and southeast New Mexico. On Tuesday, an upper-level \ntrough will be making its way towards the region. Simultaneously, \nthe remnants of Hurricane Polo are pushed our direction as well. As \nthis occurs, both lift and moisture increase across the area. In \nfact, models indicate PWATs will be pushing daily maximum values, \nsitting between 1.5-1.8 inches (up to 2.0 inches) for most \nlocations. As a result, medium to high (40-80%) rain chances are \nforecast across much of the area Tuesday into Wednesday. The highest \nchances (60-80%) are still maintained for southeast New Mexico and \nthe Permian Basin, which will be relatively closer to the best lift. \nGiven the quality of moisture and how much rain some portions of the \narea of seen in the past several days, flash flooding is going to be \nthe primary concern. This will be especially true for southeast New \nMexico and Culberson County, where the Flood Watch mentioned in the \nShort Term discussion will remain in effect through early Wednesday \nmorning (though this watch may be expanded in future forecast \nupdates as confidence increases). \n\nThe duration of this event is still somewhat questionable. The GFS \nhas tended to be more progressive with the trough (moving the best \nrain chances off to our east by Wednesday afternoon) whereas the \nECMWF has been slower (keeping rain chances in play through Thursday \nafternoon). In any case, the overall pattern bears some similarities \nto October 21-22nd of 2015, where a trough interacted with tropical \nmoisture and yielded flash flooding across southeast New Mexico and \nmuch of the Permian Basin. Incidentally, that event also occurred \nduring an El Nino. Beyond our rain chances, guidance indicates a \nfront will move across our area during the latter half of the week. \nOnce again, differences in the exact timing and magnitude of the \nfront exist within the models. Nevertheless, it looks like by the \nend of the week highs in the mid-to-upper 70s and low 80s become \ncommonplace (versus the mid-to-upper 80s and low 90s forecast for \nTuesday).\n\nSprang",
